\title{neuralign: General-Purpose Alignment Infrastructure}
\description{
\code{neuralign} provides a domain-agnostic framework for fitting and applying
alignment transforms across a set of subjects (or other grouped datasets).
It is designed to centralize common alignment operations so that downstream
packages can reuse consistent implementations of alignment, cross-validation,
feature harmonization, and provenance tracking.
}
\details{
\subsection{Core conventions}{

\strong{Data matrices.} By convention, subject data matrices are oriented as
\strong{features × observations}. If your data are in the opposite orientation,
transpose them before creating an \link{AlignmentData} object.

\strong{Transforms.} Most aligners return linear operators that are applied by
\strong{left-multiplication} (e.g., \code{Q \%*\% X}). When a right-multiplication
convention is needed (e.g., \code{X \%*\% Q}), use a convention-aware helper such as
\code{\link[=procrustes_rotation]{procrustes_rotation()}} or transpose the problem explicitly.
}

\subsection{Key components}{
\itemize{
\item \link{AlignmentData}: container for per-subject matrices (and optional observation
labels, guidance, and other metadata).
\item \code{\link[=fit_alignment]{fit_alignment()}} / \code{\link[=apply_alignment]{apply_alignment()}}: fit an alignment model and apply it
to new subjects or held-out data.
\item \link{AlignmentModel} / \link{AlignmentResult}: standardized objects for storing
fitted transforms, aligned outputs, and quality/CV metadata.
\item \link{AlignedStudy} / \link{SharedFeatureSpace}: analysis-facing aligned data with
observation metadata, shared-space identity, lineage, and safety records
(see \code{\link[=align_study]{align_study()}}, \code{\link[=as_aligned_study]{as_aligned_study()}}).
\item Feature blocks: represent heterogeneous alignment signals and harmonize them
across subjects via shared feature names (see \code{\link[=alignment_feature_block]{alignment_feature_block()}},
\code{\link[=harmonize_feature_blocks]{harmonize_feature_blocks()}}, \code{\link[=stack_feature_blocks]{stack_feature_blocks()}}).
}
}

\subsection{Cross-validation}{

\code{neuralign} supports both subject-axis and observation-axis cross-validation.
Use \code{\link[=create_cv_folds]{create_cv_folds()}} for subject CV and \code{\link[=create_obs_folds]{create_obs_folds()}} for
observation-axis folds (e.g., run-based or blocked-time splits).
}

\subsection{Preprocessing}{

\code{neuralign} does not perform centering, scaling, or other preprocessing by
default. Apply any desired preprocessing to your matrices before fitting.
}
